<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-coefficients-in-binomial-expansions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Coefficients in Binomial Expansions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.2 Binomial Expansions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine coefficients of terms in binomial expansions up to the power of 10
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Building Pascal's triangle (to the 11th row) to find coefficients of terms up to power 10.
         Suggested activity: Learners build Pascal's triangle to the 11th row using bottle tops or number cards on the floor, then read off coefficients for (a+b)¹⁰.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Pascal's triangle that builds row by row with a slider; clicking a row highlights the matching coefficients in (a+b)ⁿ. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (b). Lessons 2.1 and 2.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (bottle tops or number cards, to the row for power 10, the design's 11th row)
         JSXGraph: interactive-pascals-triangle | as planning notes (slider builds the rows; the row for power n is boxed and (a + b)^n written out; clicking an inside number shows the two numbers that make it)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/02-binomial-expansions/02-coefficients-in-binomial-expansions/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pascals-triangle">
        <title>Pascal's Triangle</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> Pascal's triangle and its addition rule; the row for power <m>n</m> gives the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^n</m>; the numbers in the row for power <m>n</m> add up to <m>2^n</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> build the rows up to power 10; expand <m>(a + b)^n</m> and <m>(p + qx)^n</m> for <m>n</m> up to 10 (in full to power 7, the first terms beyond).</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find and explain patterns in the triangle.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners build Pascal's triangle to the row for power 10 and read off the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^{10}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Groups of four lay out bottle tops or number cards on the floor, or fill in the classroom Pascal's triangle chart. Give each learner a role: one adds, one checks, one records and one places the cards; rotate roles after every two rows (collaboration). Ask <q>Why does the rule work?</q> only after part (c) (critical thinking).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-bottle-top-triangle">
            <title>A Bottle-Top Triangle</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Work in a group of four with bottle tops (write a number on each with a marker) or number cards.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Lay out the rows of coefficients for powers 0 to 4 from <xref ref="act-multiplying-out-on-manila-paper"/>, with a single 1 at the top for power 0, since <m>(a + b)^0 = 1</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>1; 1, 1; 1, 2, 1; 1, 3, 3, 1; 1, 4, 6, 4, 1, centred one under another.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Each new number is the sum of the two numbers above it, and each row starts and ends with 1. Build the triangle down to the row for power 10 (that is 11 rows in all).</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Power 5: 1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1. Power 6: 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1. Power 7: 1, 7, 21, 35, 35, 21, 7, 1. Power 8: 1, 8, 28, 56, 70, 56, 28, 8, 1. Power 9: 1, 9, 36, 84, 126, 126, 84, 36, 9, 1. Power 10: 1, 10, 45, 120, 210, 252, 210, 120, 45, 10, 1.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Add the numbers in each row. What do you notice?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The sums are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, ..., 1024: each sum is double the one before, so the row for power <m>n</m> adds up to <m>2^n</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Write down the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^{10}</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>1, 10, 45, 120, 210, 252, 210, 120, 45, 10, 1.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find a slanting line of numbers that are the counting numbers 1, 2, 3, ..., and a slanting line that reads 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The second slanting line (just inside the edge of 1s) gives 1, 2, 3, 4, ...; the third gives 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, ...</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise Pascal's triangle and its link with the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^n</m>.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> name the rows by power: the single 1 is the row for power 0, so the row for power 10 is the 11th row.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The addition rule builds every row from the one above; the row for power <m>n</m> gives the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^n</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Keep the triangle (or the chart) on the wall: it is used in every lesson to the end of the chapter.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-pascals-triangle">
            <title>Pascal's Triangle</title>
            <p>
                <term>Pascal's triangle</term> is built row by row: every row starts and ends with 1, and each number inside is the sum of the two numbers above it. We name each row by its power: the single 1 at the top is the row for power 0, and the row for power <m>n</m> gives the coefficients of <m>(a + b)^n</m>. The row for power 10 is the 11th row if you count the top 1 as the first.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each row reads the same forwards and backwards, and the numbers in the row for power <m>n</m> add up to <m>2^n</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The triangle is named after the French mathematician Blaise Pascal (1623 to 1662), but it was known centuries earlier to mathematicians in China, India and Persia.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-pascals-triangle-to-row-10">
